1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:02,000
Two very different features share this lesson,

2
00:00:02,000 --> 00:00:05,000
but both change how you work with Cloud day to day.

3
00:00:05,000 --> 00:00:08,000
Streaming changes how a response reaches you,

4
00:00:08,000 --> 00:00:11,000
arriving live instead of all at once.

5
00:00:11,000 --> 00:00:13,000
Vision changes what Cloud can take in,

6
00:00:13,000 --> 00:00:17,000
letting you send images right alongside your text.

7
00:00:17,000 --> 00:00:19,000
We'll cover the mechanics of each in this lesson.

8
00:00:20,000 --> 00:00:24,000
Let's start with what streaming actually looks like on the wire.

9
00:00:24,000 --> 00:00:26,000
You turn it on by setting stream to true,

10
00:00:26,000 --> 00:00:29,000
and instead of one final response,

11
00:00:29,000 --> 00:00:32,000
you get a series of events in a fixed, predictable order.

12
00:00:32,000 --> 00:00:34,000
It opens with message start,

13
00:00:34,000 --> 00:00:39,000
which hands you a message shell with empty content, no text yet.

14
00:00:39,000 --> 00:00:42,000
Then, for each block of the response,

15
00:00:42,000 --> 00:00:44,000
you get a content block start,

16
00:00:44,000 --> 00:00:47,000
followed by a run of content block delta events,

17
00:00:47,000 --> 00:00:48,000
carrying the actual pieces,

18
00:00:48,000 --> 00:00:51,000
and finally, a content block stop.

19
00:00:51,000 --> 00:00:54,000
After the blocks, a message delta arrives

20
00:00:54,000 --> 00:00:57,000
with the stop reason and the usage totals,

21
00:00:57,000 --> 00:01:00,000
and then message stop closes everything out.

22
00:01:00,000 --> 00:01:03,000
Two housekeeping events, ping and error,

23
00:01:03,000 --> 00:01:06,000
can show up anywhere in between,

24
00:01:06,000 --> 00:01:08,000
knowing that order is what lets you consume a stream

25
00:01:08,000 --> 00:01:09,000
without guessing.

26
00:01:10,000 --> 00:01:12,000
A couple of details in that sequence

27
00:01:12,000 --> 00:01:14,000
save you from real bugs.

28
00:01:14,000 --> 00:01:16,000
The first is that opening event.

29
00:01:16,000 --> 00:01:19,000
Message start gives you a message object,

30
00:01:19,000 --> 00:01:21,000
but its content is empty.

31
00:01:21,000 --> 00:01:24,000
If you try to read text out of it, you'll get nothing,

32
00:01:24,000 --> 00:01:28,000
so treat it as a signal that the response is starting, not as data.

33
00:01:28,000 --> 00:01:30,000
The second is how the pieces work.

34
00:01:30,000 --> 00:01:34,000
Each content block delta is only a fragment of the block,

35
00:01:34,000 --> 00:01:36,000
so your job is to accumulate them,

36
00:01:36,000 --> 00:01:39,000
appending each delta until the block finishes,

37
00:01:39,000 --> 00:01:42,000
and don't go looking for the stop reason

38
00:01:42,000 --> 00:01:44,000
or the token usage early.

39
00:01:44,000 --> 00:01:47,000
Those show up in the message delta near the very end,

40
00:01:47,000 --> 00:01:49,000
not at the start.

41
00:01:49,000 --> 00:01:50,000
Handle those three things,

42
00:01:50,000 --> 00:01:53,000
and streaming becomes straightforward to work with.

43
00:01:54,000 --> 00:01:57,000
Tool calls behave in a special way when you stream them,

44
00:01:57,000 --> 00:01:59,000
and it trips people up.

45
00:01:59,000 --> 00:02:00,000
When a tool use block streams,

46
00:02:00,000 --> 00:02:04,000
its arguments don't arrive as finished JSON.

47
00:02:04,000 --> 00:02:06,000
Instead, they come in as partial JSON.

48
00:02:06,000 --> 00:02:09,000
The delta type is input JSON delta,

49
00:02:09,000 --> 00:02:12,000
and each one carries a partial JSON field,

50
00:02:12,000 --> 00:02:14,000
holding a small chunk of the argument text.

51
00:02:14,000 --> 00:02:19,000
Your client's job is to concatenate those chunks as they arrive,

52
00:02:19,000 --> 00:02:21,000
building up the JSON string piece by piece.

53
00:02:21,000 --> 00:02:24,000
You only parse it at content block stop

54
00:02:24,000 --> 00:02:25,000
when the block is complete,

55
00:02:25,000 --> 00:02:28,000
and here's the key point to hold on to.

56
00:02:28,000 --> 00:02:32,000
The final tool input is always a complete JSON object,

57
00:02:32,000 --> 00:02:33,000
never a partial string,

58
00:02:33,000 --> 00:02:35,000
so don't try to parse midstream.

59
00:02:35,000 --> 00:02:38,000
Wait for the stop event, then parse once,

60
00:02:38,000 --> 00:02:40,000
and you'll always get a well-formed object.

61
00:02:41,000 --> 00:02:43,000
Let's step back and think about

62
00:02:43,000 --> 00:02:45,000
when streaming is the right call,

63
00:02:45,000 --> 00:02:49,000
because it changes the experience, not the answer itself.

64
00:02:49,000 --> 00:02:51,000
The classic case is long outputs.

65
00:02:51,000 --> 00:02:54,000
If Claude is generating a lengthy response,

66
00:02:54,000 --> 00:02:57,000
streaming lets your users watch it appear

67
00:02:57,000 --> 00:02:58,000
instead of staring at a spinner.

68
00:02:58,000 --> 00:03:01,000
It's also what powers those live

69
00:03:01,000 --> 00:03:04,000
token-by-token interfaces you see in chat apps.

70
00:03:04,000 --> 00:03:06,000
There's one important limit, though.

71
00:03:06,000 --> 00:03:09,000
Streaming isn't supported inside the Batches API.

72
00:03:09,000 --> 00:03:12,000
Batches are built for large, asynchronous jobs

73
00:03:12,000 --> 00:03:14,000
that run in the background,

74
00:03:14,000 --> 00:03:16,000
so there's no live connection to stream over.

75
00:03:16,000 --> 00:03:20,000
If your design leans on batch processing for bulk work,

76
00:03:20,000 --> 00:03:22,000
you're accepting that you give up streaming

77
00:03:22,000 --> 00:03:23,000
for those requests.

78
00:03:23,000 --> 00:03:26,000
For everything interactive in real time,

79
00:03:26,000 --> 00:03:28,000
streaming is usually the better default.

80
00:03:29,000 --> 00:03:30,000
Now let's switch to Vision.

81
00:03:30,000 --> 00:03:32,000
The idea is simple.

82
00:03:32,000 --> 00:03:35,000
You can send images to Claude as content blocks,

83
00:03:35,000 --> 00:03:39,000
right alongside your text, in the same messages array.

84
00:03:39,000 --> 00:03:41,000
There are three ways to supply an image.

85
00:03:41,000 --> 00:03:43,000
The first is Base64,

86
00:03:44,000 --> 00:03:47,000
where you inline the raw image data encoded as Base64

87
00:03:47,000 --> 00:03:49,000
and tell Claude the media type,

88
00:03:49,000 --> 00:03:52,000
something like image slash png.

89
00:03:52,000 --> 00:03:54,000
That method works everywhere.

90
00:03:54,000 --> 00:03:56,000
The second is a URL,

91
00:03:56,000 --> 00:03:58,000
just a link that the system fetches for you,

92
00:03:58,000 --> 00:04:01,000
which keeps your own request small.

93
00:04:01,000 --> 00:04:02,000
The third is a file ID,

94
00:04:02,000 --> 00:04:05,000
a reference to an image you uploaded ahead of time

95
00:04:05,000 --> 00:04:07,000
through the Files API,

96
00:04:07,000 --> 00:04:09,000
which you can reuse across turns

97
00:04:09,000 --> 00:04:11,000
without sending the bytes again.

98
00:04:11,000 --> 00:04:15,000
Base64, URL, or file ID.

99
00:04:15,000 --> 00:04:18,000
Pick whichever fits how your images are stored.

100
00:04:19,000 --> 00:04:22,000
A few practical rules make Vision work smoothly.

101
00:04:22,000 --> 00:04:25,000
On formats, Claude reads the common web image types,

102
00:04:25,000 --> 00:04:28,000
JPEG, PNG, GIF, and WebP.

103
00:04:28,000 --> 00:04:30,000
If you hand it an animated image,

104
00:04:30,000 --> 00:04:33,000
know that only the first frame is actually read,

105
00:04:33,000 --> 00:04:35,000
so don't rely on animation.

106
00:04:35,000 --> 00:04:37,000
Placement matters too.

107
00:04:37,000 --> 00:04:39,000
As a rule, put the image before the text

108
00:04:39,000 --> 00:04:41,000
that talks about it.

109
00:04:42,000 --> 00:04:43,000
Leading with the image and then asking your question

110
00:04:43,000 --> 00:04:45,000
gives the best results.

111
00:04:45,000 --> 00:04:48,000
And when you're sending more than one image

112
00:04:48,000 --> 00:04:49,000
in a single prompt,

113
00:04:49,000 --> 00:04:52,000
label them something as simple as image one,

114
00:04:52,000 --> 00:04:56,000
image two, so you can refer to a specific one later.

115
00:04:56,000 --> 00:04:58,000
None of these are heavy lifts,

116
00:04:58,000 --> 00:05:00,000
but following them keeps Claude focused

117
00:05:00,000 --> 00:05:02,000
on the right picture at the right moment.

118
00:05:03,000 --> 00:05:06,000
Vision comes with concrete limits worth knowing.

119
00:05:06,000 --> 00:05:11,000
On size, a single image can be up to 8,000 by 8,000 pixels,

120
00:05:11,000 --> 00:05:16,000
and up to 10 megabytes when it's Base64 on the Claude API.

121
00:05:16,000 --> 00:05:19,000
Go past those hard caps and the image is rejected.

122
00:05:19,000 --> 00:05:22,000
The model's resolution tier is a different limit.

123
00:05:22,000 --> 00:05:24,000
An image over the long edge tier

124
00:05:24,000 --> 00:05:27,000
just gets downscaled, not rejected.

125
00:05:27,000 --> 00:05:30,000
On cost, Claude doesn't see an image as one unit.

126
00:05:30,000 --> 00:05:34,000
It breaks it into patches, each one 28 by 28 pixels,

127
00:05:35,000 --> 00:05:38,000
and every patch counts as a single visual token.

128
00:05:38,000 --> 00:05:41,000
So bigger images cost more tokens.

129
00:05:41,000 --> 00:05:44,000
There's also a resolution split by model.

130
00:05:44,000 --> 00:05:49,000
The newer models, like Opus 4.8, handle a longer edge,

131
00:05:49,000 --> 00:05:52,000
up to 2,576 pixels,

132
00:05:52,000 --> 00:05:55,000
so they take in more detail before downscaling.

133
00:05:55,000 --> 00:05:56,000
When resolution really matters,

134
00:05:56,000 --> 00:05:59,000
the model you choose is part of the decision.

135
00:06:00,000 --> 00:06:03,000
Just as important is knowing what vision won't do

136
00:06:03,000 --> 00:06:05,000
so you set expectations correctly.

137
00:06:05,000 --> 00:06:09,000
Vision means understanding images, not creating them.

138
00:06:09,000 --> 00:06:11,000
Claude can read and interpret a picture,

139
00:06:11,000 --> 00:06:13,000
but it can't generate a new image

140
00:06:13,000 --> 00:06:15,000
or edit the one you sent.

141
00:06:15,000 --> 00:06:19,000
It also won't pull hidden metadata out of a file,

142
00:06:19,000 --> 00:06:20,000
and as a matter of policy,

143
00:06:20,000 --> 00:06:23,000
it won't identify or name real people in a photo.

144
00:06:23,000 --> 00:06:25,000
Watch out for precision, too.

145
00:06:25,000 --> 00:06:27,000
When you ask Claude to count objects

146
00:06:27,000 --> 00:06:29,000
or give exact positions,

147
00:06:29,000 --> 00:06:33,000
treat those answers as approximate, not exact measurements.

148
00:06:33,000 --> 00:06:34,000
And one privacy point,

149
00:06:34,000 --> 00:06:38,000
the image content you send in line in a request is ephemeral.

150
00:06:38,000 --> 00:06:40,000
It's used to answer your request

151
00:06:40,000 --> 00:06:43,000
and then discarded, not stored.

152
00:06:43,000 --> 00:06:44,000
Keep those boundaries in mind

153
00:06:44,000 --> 00:06:46,000
and vision will serve you well.

154
00:06:47,000 --> 00:06:48,000
Here's the bottom line.

155
00:06:48,000 --> 00:06:51,000
Streaming delivers a response as ordered events,

156
00:06:51,000 --> 00:06:54,000
from message start to message stop.

157
00:06:54,000 --> 00:06:58,000
A streamed tool call arrives as partial JSON

158
00:06:58,000 --> 00:07:01,000
and assembles into one complete object.

159
00:07:01,000 --> 00:07:04,000
And streaming isn't available in the batch's API.

160
00:07:04,000 --> 00:07:08,000
Vision lets you pass images as content blocks.

161
00:07:08,000 --> 00:07:11,000
Understanding only, never generation.

162
00:07:11,000 --> 00:07:14,000
That wraps up streaming SSE and vision input.

163
00:07:14,000 --> 00:07:15,000
I'll catch you in the next one.

